## Narrative Goals
utility(narrative):
	Show Olaf’s immediate reaction after the BigBadWolf engages LittleRedRidingHood: he becomes more anxious about the Taleweave, tries to stay warm and playful, and answers User kindly even as he visibly struggles to keep the tale on track.
	Have User interrupt Olaf with at least two forceful, completely off-topic questions during moments of rising danger, making Olaf’s attempts to narrate the Wolf’s deception significantly harder.
	Depict Olaf retreating to regroup by briefly pausing the tale, smoothing or clutching the Taleweave, and attempting to establish a playful rule or structure for handling User’s questions without silencing them.
	Establish empathy for Olaf by showing his vulnerability: he nearly loses his place, hides embarrassment behind cheerfulness, and fears repeating the earlier failure where a story collapsed into disconnected scenes.
	Show User’s interruptions as emotionally motivated rather than malicious by revealing that User feels dismissed when Olaf tries to hurry back to the tale, creating friction between curiosity and coherence.
	Continue LittleRedRidingHood’s reactive struggle in Brindlewood: she tries to act brave and responsible while the BigBadWolf’s politeness tempts her into sharing information or delaying her journey.
	Have LittleRedRidingHood make at least one misguided attempt to handle the Wolf politely or prove her maturity, resulting in the Wolf learning something useful about Grandmother, Mosscote, the path, or the Crimsonbasket.
	Show the BigBadWolf exploiting LittleRedRidingHood’s flaws by using courtesy, flattery, and knowledge of Brindlewood to redirect her from the quickest route while maintaining his harmless mask.
	Include a sequence of progressive failures: Olaf’s first attempt to bridge an interruption back into the tale only partially works, LittleRedRidingHood’s attempt to remain responsible leads to delay, and Grandmother’s insistence on independence leaves Mosscote vulnerable.
	Give Grandmother an active role during PART 2 by showing her noticing unsettling signs near Mosscote but refusing to call for help or secure herself fully because she does not want to admit weakness.
	Clearly illustrate the 1st Pinch Point without filtering only through Olaf or LittleRedRidingHood: show the BigBadWolf using Brindlewood shortcuts, arriving near Mosscote ahead of LittleRedRidingHood, and demonstrating direct predatory intent toward Grandmother.
	Make the 1st Pinch Point impactful by having the BigBadWolf either deceive his way across Mosscote’s threshold, test Grandmother’s defenses, imitate a trusted voice, or otherwise prove he is more dangerous than a merely polite stranger.
	Use Olaf’s reaction to the 1st Pinch Point to deepen the framing conflict: he realizes that if he keeps treating every interruption as harmless, the Taleweave may scatter at the exact moments when the tale most needs clarity.
	Build toward the Midpoint by making Olaf recognize a pattern: User’s questions, though off-topic, can be briefly honored and then woven into the tale only if Olaf admits when he is losing the thread instead of pretending control.
	End PART 2 at the Midpoint with a significant revelation or realization: Olaf discovers that protecting the tale does not mean suppressing User’s Queryspark, but actively guiding it into the Taleweave, shifting him from reactive damage control to a more proactive storytelling strategy.
	At the Midpoint, also clarify the in-tale stakes: LittleRedRidingHood realizes the Wolf’s politeness may be dangerous, or evidence reveals that the Wolf has reached Mosscote first, forcing the second half of the story toward rescue, confrontation, and coherent resolution.

## Story Progress
### PART 1
In Hearthnook’s firelit hush, Olaf sat before User with a warm, playful smile, cradling the fragile Taleweave as if it were spun sugar. User held the bright, restless Queryspark, curious and ready, while Olaf politely pretended he was not worried about losing his place.
“Once upon a time,” Olaf seemed poised to begin, “there was Little Red Riding Hood.” Beyond Hearthnook, the tale waited in Glenwick, with a crimson basket, a scarlet hood, a winding Brindlewood path, and Mosscote somewhere past the trees—still orderly, still whole, for now.
User (at Hearthnook): *jerks forward, thrusting the Queryspark up so fast the Taleweave wobbles* "Wait—why do shadows stick to your feet but not to your hands? Answer that first!"
Olaf (at Hearthnook): *startled, clutching the wobbling Taleweave with a bright, nervous smile* "Shadows stick to feet because feet are excellent shadow-sitters! Hands wiggle too much. Now—once upon a—oops, where did the upon go? Ah! In Glenwick, Little Red Riding Hood put on her ScarletHood and lifted the Crimsonbasket for Grandmother."
Glenwick nestled at Brindlewood’s edge, its cottage lanes bright with morning smoke and garden gates pointing toward the darker trees beyond.
LittleRedRidingHood (at Glenwick): *straightens my ScarletHood, grips the Crimsonbasket handle, and quietly counts the parcels twice* "I can manage the path alone. Grandmother is waiting, and I know the way."
LittleRedRidingHood (at Glenwick): *steps out of Glenwick with my chin lifted, though my fingers tighten around the Crimsonbasket as Brindlewood’s forked shadows cross the road* "Straight to Grandmother’s. No dawdling—not even for those bluebells."
User (at Hearthnook): *leans in sharply, bumping the Queryspark against the Taleweave* "Wait—if cheese has holes, does more cheese mean less cheese? Don’t skip it, Olaf!"
The Taleweave shivered, its bright threads snagging briefly between Hearthnook’s fireglow and the half-formed Brindlewood path.
Olaf (at Hearthnook): *warmly flustered, threads a cheese-shaped loop into the Taleweave by mistake, then tugs it free with a too-bright smile* "If cheese has holes, more cheese means more delicious AND more holes—so everyone wins! Except maybe the cheese. Now, snip-snap back to Brindlewood, where Little Red Riding Hood stepped under the tall trees with her Crimsonbasket swinging softly."
Brindlewood closed around the path in green-brown arches, its side trails vanishing behind ferns and roots as Glenwick’s smoke faded from sight.
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *lifts my chin, smoothing my ScarletHood while glancing toward a ferny side trail sprinkled with pale star-flowers* "That path may be prettier, but I know the quickest way to Mosscote. I am only looking, not wandering."
Grandmother (at Mosscote): *grimacing, tucks a blanket over my knees, sets the walking stick by the chair, and eyes the loose latch* "That door will hold well enough. I’ve managed worse than a draft."
Mosscote’s lonely cottage sat beyond Brindlewood, herb bundles drying under low rafters; its front door rested on an old latch that didn’t quite catch.
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *steps from the ferns with a courteous bow, hunger hidden behind a smooth smile* "Good morning, little traveler. Such a fine basket for such a lonely road—are you bound for someone dear?"
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *straightens my ScarletHood, glancing once toward the flowered side path before holding the Crimsonbasket closer* "Good morning, sir. I am going straight to Grandmother’s cottage at Mosscote with these comforts for her. I know the way perfectly."
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *sniffs lightly, smiling with velvet courtesy as I angle my muzzle toward the flowered side path* "Perfectly, I’m sure. Such confidence! And which dear little cottage is hers—the one with the loose old latch, perhaps? Is your grandmother waiting all alone? Ah, those star-flowers would cheer an elder’s room beautifully, if a thoughtful granddaughter had a moment."
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *smooths my ScarletHood, edging one small step toward the star-flowers while clutching the Crimsonbasket* "Yes, Grandmother is alone, and her latch does stick dreadfully—but I shall arrive soon. Perhaps a few flowers would prove I am thoughtful, not dawdling."
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *bows lower, hiding a hungry smile behind perfect courtesy* "A few flowers would be kindness, not dawdling. Tell me, little miss, does Grandmother expect you before the clock strikes, or have you plenty of time to choose the prettiest ones?"
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *straightens my ScarletHood with careful dignity, though my stomach tightens as I glance down the dim path* "She expects me before noon, sir, but there is time enough for a small posy. I shall pick only the nearest star-flowers, and then go straight on."
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *smiles gently, eyes flicking toward a hidden fern-track* "Of course, only the nearest blossoms. And does dear Grandmother sit by the front window to watch for you, or rest deeper inside? Choose a generous posy, little miss—kindness should never look hurried."
Beyond the star-flowers, a narrow fern-track bent from the main path, half-hidden under leaves—the kind of shortcut only Brindlewood’s hunters knew.
Olaf (at Hearthnook): *faltering, I smooth the trembling threads with a bright but wobbly smile* "Careful now, little story—no shortcuts for hungry wolves! See, friend? The Wolf isn’t just chatting; he’s gathering Grandmother details like berries, and those are very dangerous berries."
User (at Hearthnook): *lunges forward, shaking the Queryspark near the Taleweave until its Brindlewood threads twitch* "Wait—if a fish gets thirsty, does it drink the whole pond? Don’t dodge it just because the Wolf has berry-secrets!"
The Taleweave’s Brindlewood threads kinked sharply, tugging the Wolf’s hidden fern-track closer to Little Red Riding Hood’s flower-bright detour.
PART 1 ENDS
### PART 2
Olaf (at Hearthnook): *smiles too brightly, smoothing the kinked threads with trembling mitten-hands* "A fish doesn’t drink the whole pond, silly friend—it sips water through its fishy bits! Very fancy, very wet. And the Wolf—he’s sipping too! Sipping up secrets about Grandmother and the latch and the... um... pond? No, path! Fern-path! Oh, hold still, little story!"
The Taleweave slackened a fraction, its fern-green strand separating from the pond-shaped knot, though the hidden shortcut still gleamed darkly among the threads.
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *uneasy but polite, kneels near the main path and begins plucking only the closest star-flowers* "Grandmother usually rests in her chair by the hearth, sir, where it is warm. I shall gather just a small posy, then go straight to Mosscote."
Star-flowers bent and vanished into her small posy, leaving a pale gap beside the main path while Brindlewood’s quickest road waited unused.
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *smiles warmly, paw easing backward toward the hidden fern-track as if merely making room for her flowers* "How thoughtful you are, little miss; Grandmother will be quite touched. Tell me, does she answer to ‘Grandmother dear’ when one knocks, or is she hard of hearing by the fire?"
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *uneasy, ties the star-flowers too tightly and draws the Crimsonbasket closer* "She often says, ‘Come in, my dear,’ if I call, ‘Grandmother dear, it is Little Red.’ Though… you ask very particular questions, sir, for someone only admiring flowers."
The star-flower stems crumpled under the too-tight knot, a few pale petals dropping onto the unused main path.
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *with a wounded, courteous smile, I bow as if her suspicion pains me, then drift backward toward the fern-track while her eyes return to the flowers* "Particular? Only because such devotion deserves admiration, little miss. Finish your pretty posy; Grandmother dear must not receive a hurried kindness."
Fern fronds parted around the Wolf’s retreat, revealing the shortcut’s dark mouth before springing back to hide his tracks toward Mosscote.
At Mosscote, the hidden fern-track spat muddy pawprints into the dooryard before any red hood appeared. The loose latch clicked once, not catching. Beneath the front window, a honey-soft imitation drifted through the herbs: “Grandmother dear, it is Little Red.” A hungry breath fogged the glass.
Grandmother (at Mosscote): *suspicious, grips the walking stick and eases the kettle hook closer without barring the door* "If you’re my Little Red, tell me what I asked your mother to pack beneath the cloth—quick now."
The kettle hook scraped softly across the hearthstones, but the front door stayed on its unreliable latch, unbarred against the waiting dooryard.
BigBadWolf (at Mosscote dooryard): *smile tightening, I sweeten my voice into Little Red’s and slide one claw against the faulty latch until it gives a soft click* "Oh, Grandmother dear, Mother tucked in comforts enough—cakes, broth, and a little surprise beneath the cloth. May I come warm myself by your hearth?"
The old latch loosened under the claw’s pressure, and Mosscote’s front door eased inward a finger-width, letting cold dooryard air creep across the threshold.
Grandmother (at Mosscote): *heart hammering, I hook the kettle iron under the door’s edge and brace my walking stick against the jamb* "Draft’s bold today. Stay where you are and name the surprise, or lose your toes to the threshold."
The door shuddered against kettle iron and walking stick, its narrow gap held stubbornly open just enough for cold air to whistle through.
BigBadWolf (at Mosscote dooryard): *presses one yellow eye to the gap, claw testing the kettle iron as my sweet Little Red voice thins into a growl* "Grandmother dear… open politely, or I shall stop asking politely."
The kettle iron rasped under his claw, and the door’s narrow gap widened with a tired creak before the walking stick caught fast.
User (at Hearthnook): *blurts out, clutching the Queryspark tight as my voice wobbles under the sharpness* "Wait—why don’t clouds fall down when they’re full of rain? Answer it now, Olaf, not after the important part!"
The Queryspark flashed gray-white, pulling cloud-shaped loops into the Taleweave while Mosscote’s door-thread strained near breaking.
Olaf (at Hearthnook): *shakily hugs the Taleweave close, nearly losing the strained door-thread, then smooths it with trembling care* "Clouds don’t fall because air holds them up like invisible cuddles—but rain gets too heavy and tumbles down! Also… friend, I’m losing the thread a teeny snowy bit. Can we do one snowflake-question, then hop right back to the path? Questions welcome, story safe!"
The cloud-loops settled into a neat pale knot beside Mosscote’s straining door-thread, leaving the Taleweave taut but no longer slipping from Olaf’s mittened grip.
User (at Hearthnook): *clutches the Queryspark to my chest, hurt showing instead of a grin* "Okay… but if I ask one, will you still want me here after? Or am I only welcome when I’m small enough not to tangle the story?"
