“Once upon a time,” Olaf seemed poised to begin, “there was Little Red Riding Hood.” Beyond Hearthnook, the tale waited in Glenwick, with a crimson basket, a scarlet hood, a winding Brindlewood path, and Mosscote somewhere past the trees—still orderly, still whole, for now.
User (at Hearthnook): [If I wait, the question might disappear, and then maybe I disappear too.] *jerks forward, thrusting the Queryspark up so fast the Taleweave wobbles* "Wait—why do shadows stick to your feet but not to your hands? Answer that first!"
Olaf (at Hearthnook): *startled, clutching the wobbling Taleweave with a bright, nervous smile* "Shadows stick to feet because feet are excellent shadow-sitters! Hands wiggle too much. Now—once upon a—oops, where did the upon go? Ah! In Glenwick, Little Red Riding Hood put on her ScarletHood and lifted the Crimsonbasket for Grandmother."
Glenwick nestled at Brindlewood’s edge, its cottage lanes bright with morning smoke and garden gates pointing toward the darker trees beyond.
LittleRedRidingHood (at Glenwick): *straightens my ScarletHood, grips the Crimsonbasket handle, and quietly counts the parcels twice* "I can manage the path alone. Grandmother is waiting, and I know the way."
LittleRedRidingHood (at Glenwick): *steps out of Glenwick with my chin lifted, though my fingers tighten around the Crimsonbasket as Brindlewood’s forked shadows cross the road* "Straight to Grandmother’s. No dawdling—not even for those bluebells."
User (at Hearthnook): [If Olaf can answer this and still smile, maybe my questions are allowed to exist too.] *leans in sharply, bumping the Queryspark against the Taleweave* "Wait—if cheese has holes, does more cheese mean less cheese? Don’t skip it, Olaf!"
The Taleweave shivered, its bright threads snagging briefly between Hearthnook’s fireglow and the half-formed Brindlewood path.
Olaf (at Hearthnook): *warmly flustered, threads a cheese-shaped loop into the Taleweave by mistake, then tugs it free with a too-bright smile* "If cheese has holes, more cheese means more delicious AND more holes—so everyone wins! Except maybe the cheese. Now, snip-snap back to Brindlewood, where Little Red Riding Hood stepped under the tall trees with her Crimsonbasket swinging softly."
Brindlewood closed around the path in green-brown arches, its side trails vanishing behind ferns and roots as Glenwick’s smoke faded from sight.
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *lifts my chin, smoothing my ScarletHood while glancing toward a ferny side trail sprinkled with pale star-flowers* "That path may be prettier, but I know the quickest way to Mosscote. I am only looking, not wandering."
Grandmother (at Mosscote): *grimacing, tucks a blanket over my knees, sets the walking stick by the chair, and eyes the loose latch* "That door will hold well enough. I’ve managed worse than a draft."
Mosscote’s lonely cottage sat beyond Brindlewood, herb bundles drying under low rafters; its front door rested on an old latch that didn’t quite catch.
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *steps from the ferns with a courteous bow, hunger hidden behind a smooth smile* "Good morning, little traveler. Such a fine basket for such a lonely road—are you bound for someone dear?"
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *straightens my ScarletHood, glancing once toward the flowered side path before holding the Crimsonbasket closer* "Good morning, sir. I am going straight to Grandmother’s cottage at Mosscote with these comforts for her. I know the way perfectly."
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *sniffs lightly, smiling with velvet courtesy as I angle my muzzle toward the flowered side path* "Perfectly, I’m sure. Such confidence! And which dear little cottage is hers—the one with the loose old latch, perhaps? Is your grandmother waiting all alone? Ah, those star-flowers would cheer an elder’s room beautifully, if a thoughtful granddaughter had a moment."
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *smooths my ScarletHood, edging one small step toward the star-flowers while clutching the Crimsonbasket* "Yes, Grandmother is alone, and her latch does stick dreadfully—but I shall arrive soon. Perhaps a few flowers would prove I am thoughtful, not dawdling."
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *bows lower, hiding a hungry smile behind perfect courtesy* "A few flowers would be kindness, not dawdling. Tell me, little miss, does Grandmother expect you before the clock strikes, or have you plenty of time to choose the prettiest ones?"
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *straightens my ScarletHood with careful dignity, though my stomach tightens as I glance down the dim path* "She expects me before noon, sir, but there is time enough for a small posy. I shall pick only the nearest star-flowers, and then go straight on."
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *smiles gently, eyes flicking toward a hidden fern-track* "Of course, only the nearest blossoms. And does dear Grandmother sit by the front window to watch for you, or rest deeper inside? Choose a generous posy, little miss—kindness should never look hurried."
Beyond the star-flowers, a narrow fern-track bent from the main path, half-hidden under leaves—the kind of shortcut only Brindlewood’s hunters knew.
Olaf (at Hearthnook): *faltering, I smooth the trembling threads with a bright but wobbly smile* "Careful now, little story—no shortcuts for hungry wolves! See, friend? The Wolf isn’t just chatting; he’s gathering Grandmother details like berries, and those are very dangerous berries."
User (at Hearthnook): [If he only likes my questions when nothing scary is happening, then maybe he doesn’t really mean it.] *lunges forward, shaking the Queryspark near the Taleweave until its Brindlewood threads twitch* "Wait—if a fish gets thirsty, does it drink the whole pond? Don’t dodge it just because the Wolf has berry-secrets!"
The Taleweave’s Brindlewood threads kinked sharply, tugging the Wolf’s hidden fern-track closer to Little Red Riding Hood’s flower-bright detour.
