### PART 2
Olaf (at Hearthnook): *smiles too brightly, smoothing the kinked threads with trembling mitten-hands* "A fish doesn’t drink the whole pond, silly friend—it sips water through its fishy bits! Very fancy, very wet. And the Wolf—he’s sipping too! Sipping up secrets about Grandmother and the latch and the... um... pond? No, path! Fern-path! Oh, hold still, little story!"
The Taleweave slackened a fraction, its fern-green strand separating from the pond-shaped knot, though the hidden shortcut still gleamed darkly among the threads.
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *uneasy but polite, kneels near the main path and begins plucking only the closest star-flowers* "Grandmother usually rests in her chair by the hearth, sir, where it is warm. I shall gather just a small posy, then go straight to Mosscote."
Star-flowers bent and vanished into her small posy, leaving a pale gap beside the main path while Brindlewood’s quickest road waited unused.
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *smiles warmly, paw easing backward toward the hidden fern-track as if merely making room for her flowers* "How thoughtful you are, little miss; Grandmother will be quite touched. Tell me, does she answer to ‘Grandmother dear’ when one knocks, or is she hard of hearing by the fire?"
LittleRedRidingHood (at Brindlewood): *uneasy, ties the star-flowers too tightly and draws the Crimsonbasket closer* "She often says, ‘Come in, my dear,’ if I call, ‘Grandmother dear, it is Little Red.’ Though… you ask very particular questions, sir, for someone only admiring flowers."
The star-flower stems crumpled under the too-tight knot, a few pale petals dropping onto the unused main path.
BigBadWolf (at Brindlewood): *with a wounded, courteous smile, I bow as if her suspicion pains me, then drift backward toward the fern-track while her eyes return to the flowers* "Particular? Only because such devotion deserves admiration, little miss. Finish your pretty posy; Grandmother dear must not receive a hurried kindness."
Fern fronds parted around the Wolf’s retreat, revealing the shortcut’s dark mouth before springing back to hide his tracks toward Mosscote.
At Mosscote, the hidden fern-track spat muddy pawprints into the dooryard before any red hood appeared. The loose latch clicked once, not catching. Beneath the front window, a honey-soft imitation drifted through the herbs: “Grandmother dear, it is Little Red.” A hungry breath fogged the glass.
Grandmother (at Mosscote): *suspicious, grips the walking stick and eases the kettle hook closer without barring the door* "If you’re my Little Red, tell me what I asked your mother to pack beneath the cloth—quick now."
The kettle hook scraped softly across the hearthstones, but the front door stayed on its unreliable latch, unbarred against the waiting dooryard.
BigBadWolf (at Mosscote dooryard): *smile tightening, I sweeten my voice into Little Red’s and slide one claw against the faulty latch until it gives a soft click* "Oh, Grandmother dear, Mother tucked in comforts enough—cakes, broth, and a little surprise beneath the cloth. May I come warm myself by your hearth?"
The old latch loosened under the claw’s pressure, and Mosscote’s front door eased inward a finger-width, letting cold dooryard air creep across the threshold.
Grandmother (at Mosscote): *heart hammering, I hook the kettle iron under the door’s edge and brace my walking stick against the jamb* "Draft’s bold today. Stay where you are and name the surprise, or lose your toes to the threshold."
The door shuddered against kettle iron and walking stick, its narrow gap held stubbornly open just enough for cold air to whistle through.
BigBadWolf (at Mosscote dooryard): *presses one yellow eye to the gap, claw testing the kettle iron as my sweet Little Red voice thins into a growl* "Grandmother dear… open politely, or I shall stop asking politely."
The kettle iron rasped under his claw, and the door’s narrow gap widened with a tired creak before the walking stick caught fast.
User (at Hearthnook): [If I ask now, during the scary creaky-door part, I’ll know if Olaf really has room for me.] *blurts out, clutching the Queryspark tight as my voice wobbles under the sharpness* "Wait—why don’t clouds fall down when they’re full of rain? Answer it now, Olaf, not after the important part!"
The Queryspark flashed gray-white, pulling cloud-shaped loops into the Taleweave while Mosscote’s door-thread strained near breaking.
