## Narrative Goals
utility(narrative):
	Show Olaf shifting from reactive recovery to proactive storytelling by openly naming the danger to the Taleweave and setting a playful structure for handling User’s interruptions without silencing User.
	Have User interrupt Olaf with at least two forceful, completely off-topic questions during urgent in-tale moments, requiring Olaf to answer kindly, briefly, and then deliberately weave each interruption back into LittleRedRidingHood’s crisis.
	Demonstrate Olaf’s character growth by having him admit when he has nearly lost his place instead of pretending control, proving that honesty helps him preserve coherence better than anxious cheerfulness alone.
	Show Olaf becoming more assertive without becoming cruel: he should protect the Taleweave, maintain his warm playful voice, and make clear that User’s curiosity matters but cannot erase the tale’s danger.
	Give User active emotional friction by having them resist Olaf’s new structure at first, revealing that they fear being dismissed, while still remaining engaged enough to test whether Olaf’s warmth survives boundaries.
	Move LittleRedRidingHood from frightened reaction into active problem-solving: she should suspect the BigBadWolf’s deception, reassess what she revealed, and choose a purposeful course toward Mosscote instead of merely wandering or obeying.
	Depict LittleRedRidingHood confronting her internal fear of Brindlewood by using her observant nature to track signs of the Wolf’s shortcut, while resisting the urge to hide panic behind false grown-up confidence.
	Show Grandmother acting with agency at Mosscote by recognizing the Wolf’s intrusion or deception, attempting practical resistance, and struggling against her flaw of refusing help even when danger proves real.
	Have the BigBadWolf escalate from polite manipulation to open threat when challenged, revealing the predatory hunger beneath his courteous mask and showing that his earlier gentleness was strategic.
	Include the 2nd Pinch Point as a vivid demonstration of the BigBadWolf’s increased power: he gains decisive control inside Mosscote, endangers or confines Grandmother, and positions himself to deceive LittleRedRidingHood at close range.
	Make the 2nd Pinch Point affect both story layers: the Wolf’s near-success in Mosscote should coincide with User’s most disruptive interruption yet, forcing Olaf to hold the tale together under maximum pressure.
	Deepen the moral and emotional conflict by making Olaf choose between appeasing User completely and protecting LittleRedRidingHood’s story at the moment Grandmother is most endangered.
	Deepen LittleRedRidingHood’s emotional conflict by forcing her to confront the consequence of trusting politeness too easily and revealing information that helped the BigBadWolf reach Grandmother first.
	Deepen Grandmother’s emotional conflict by showing that her pride in independence has left her vulnerable, while still allowing her practical knowledge of Mosscote to create a clue, delay, or defensive opportunity.
	Show the BigBadWolf making a mistake caused by his flaws: hunger, overconfidence, fear of humiliation, or irritation should cause a crack in his disguise or plan that the heroes can later exploit.
	Keep the retelling coherent despite interruptions by ensuring each off-topic exchange returns to a specific story beat: LittleRedRidingHood’s approach to Mosscote, Grandmother’s danger, or the Wolf’s deception.
	End PART 3 with the Second Plot Point: reveal the final critical information needed for the climax, such as LittleRedRidingHood discovering that the figure in Grandmother’s bed is the BigBadWolf, finding Grandmother’s hidden clue, or realizing exactly how the Wolf’s disguise can be exposed.
