BigBadWolf (at Mosscote dooryard): [Enough parlor games; hunger has waited more politely than she deserves.] *snarling, I drop Little Red’s voice, wrench the kettle iron aside, and shoulder through until the stick cracks loose; in my rush, a tuft of gray fur catches on the splintered latch* "Inside, Grandmother dear. Now be still, or the next thing that breaks will not be wood."
The battered door banged inward, kettle iron skidding across the floor as the cracked walking stick left splinters under the loosened latch.
Grandmother (at Mosscote): [He’s in, then—but I won’t waste breath screaming when hands can still work.] *grim, flings bitter herbs into the hearth smoke, jabs the poker low to slow him, and scuffs the caught gray fur into a clear ash-mark by the threshold before he crowds me back* "You’ll cough before you conquer, beast. Mind the floorboards—they bite worse than I do."
Bitter smoke thickened across Mosscote’s hearthroom, while the gray tuft lay stamped in ash by the threshold like a deliberate warning.
BigBadWolf (at Mosscote): [Smoke, sticks, and old-woman tricks—enough; the child is coming, and the bed must look welcoming.] *coughing with fury, I swat the poker aside, herd Grandmother into the pantry, hook the chair beneath its latch, then snatch her cap and quilts without noticing the ash-marked fur by the door* "Rest quietly, Grandmother dear. We must make the room presentable for your devoted little visitor."
The pantry latch jammed under the wedged chair, quilts sagged toward the bed, and the ash-marked gray fur remained visible by the threshold.
